-- title:
--   Natural σ-algebra $\sigma(X_1,\dots,X_i)$ and the conditional expectations $\mathbb E_i$
-- statement:
--   Let $X_1, X_2, \dots$ be real random variables on a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mu)$. For $i\ge 0$ write $\mathbf X_{1:i}=(X_1,\dots,X_i)$ and let
--   $$
--   \mathcal F_i=\sigma(X_1,\dots,X_i)
--   $$
--   be the σ-algebra generated by the first $i$ increments. The paper's conditional expectation $\mathbb E_i(\cdot)=\mathbb E(\cdot\mid X_1,\dots,X_i)$ is the conditional expectation given $\mathcal F_i$. For $i=0$ the σ-algebra is trivial, so $\mathbb E_0=\mathbb E$.
--
--   These σ-algebras carry every conditional statement of the mission: Doob's martingale, the Lipschitz condition and the conditional moment-generating-function bound.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The sequence is indexed by $\mathbb N$ and $X_0$ is never used. `pastVec X i` is the vector $(X_1,\dots,X_i)\in\mathbb R^i$ and `natF X i` is the pull-back of the Borel σ-algebra along it. It is a sub-σ-algebra of the ambient one when $X_1,\dots,X_i$ are measurable; every statement using it assumes this.
-- source:
--   Liu, Zhang, Concentration Inequalities for Branching Random Walks with Applications to Phase Transitions in CSPs, arXiv:1601.04794 (artifact sha256 7499631b9d550f66e791c0b3df0879f26102f111fee3052e363ebbff0c72bdcb; no version stamp), §5, p. 9 (E_i(·) := E(· | X_1, ..., X_i)) and §4.2, p. 6

import Mathlib

namespace LiuZhangBRW.Concentration

/-- The vector of the first `i` increments `(X_1, …, X_i)` of a sequence `X_1, X_2, …` of real
random variables (Liu, Zhang, *Concentration Inequalities for Branching Random Walks with
Applications to Phase Transitions in CSPs*, arXiv:1601.04794, artifact sha256 7499631b…bdcb,
§5, p. 9, the notation `E_i(·) := E(· | X_1, ..., X_i)`).

**Formalization Note** The sequence is indexed by `ℕ` and the paper's `X_1, X_2, …` are
`X 1, X 2, …`; the entry `j : Fin i` of `pastVec X i ω` is `X (j + 1) ω`, so `X 0` is never
used. -/
def pastVec {Ω : Type*} (X : ℕ → Ω → ℝ) (i : ℕ) : Ω → (Fin i → ℝ) :=
  fun ω j => X (j.1 + 1) ω

set_option warn.classDefReducibility false in
/-- The natural σ-algebra `σ(X_1, …, X_i)` of the first `i` increments (Liu, Zhang,
arXiv:1601.04794, artifact sha256 7499631b…bdcb, §5, p. 9: "`E_i(·) := E(· | X_1, ..., X_i)`,
and similarly `E_{i−1}(·) := E(· | X_1, ..., X_{i−1})`"; §4.2, p. 6). Conditional expectation
`E_i` of the paper is `condExp (natF X i) μ`.

**Formalization Note** `natF X i` is the pull-back of the Borel σ-algebra of `ℝ^i` along
`(X_1, …, X_i)`. For `i = 0` it is the trivial σ-algebra `⊥`, so `E_0 = E`, as in the paper's
footnote `E_n[S_n] − E_0[S_n] = S_n − ES_n` (p. 9). It is a sub-σ-algebra of the ambient one
when `X_1, …, X_i` are measurable, which every statement using it assumes (otherwise Lean's
`condExp` returns the junk value `0`). -/
def natF {Ω : Type*} (X : ℕ → Ω → ℝ) (i : ℕ) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  MeasurableSpace.comap (pastVec X i) inferInstance

end LiuZhangBRW.Concentration


